[Music]  [Music]  the 2019 remake of The Lion King has got  us so excited but how it'd be different  from the original 1984 Animated Feature  there are plenty of fascinating  differences that only true fans will  notice so keep watching to the end to  discover the most exciting addition of  all photo realism it seems too obvious  to say the main difference between the  remake and the original is that one  looks realistic while the others in  animation but there's more to it than  that it's actually CGI  rather than real-life animals however  director Jon Favreau explained that they  wanted the remake to have a sense of  realism by borrowing techniques from Sir  David Attenborough's nature  documentaries you only have to glance at  scar Rafiki and Zazu to realize how  different the animals look in the 2019  version the hyenas the hyenas are a  major example of this focus on photo  realism the director told Empire that  the hyenas had to change a lot because  it didn't feel like they matched up well  with the photo realism Favreau explained  that a lot of the stuff around them was  very stylized in the original and  there's another difference when it comes  to hyenas originally they were called  Shenzi Banzai and edy but in the remake  they're called Shenzi as easy in Kumari  what's even more exciting is that Disney  is introducing a brand new character  into the Lion King franchise but more on  that at the end of this video  voices it's not just character names  that are different it's also the actors  voicing them no more will be Goldberger  Shenzi the new all-star cast includes  Donald Glover as Simba Beyonce as Nala  Seth Rogen as Pumbaa she would tell a  Gaea for a scar and John Cooney is  Rafiki  Favreau dish that it was wonderful to  find actors that could remind the  audience of the original performances  but also bring something new and fresh  and current however there's one iconic  voice that's the same James Earl Jones  is reprising his role as Mufasa Favreau  explained it was a way of carrying the  legacy across and keeping a connection  - the original however even that voice  is slightly different the direct  revealed the timbre of the actor's voice  had changed that served the role well  because he now sounds like a king who's  ruled for a long time no son you stay  with the other Cubs facial expressions  although we gain a lot from the remakes  photorealism we also lose some of the  nuances of the original like the facial  expressions animation supervisor Andrew  R Jones explained they had to elevate  the animal status slightly without  breaking their physicality by giving  them obviously human life gestures he  added that they did some tests and when  they pushed it too far it felt like a  cartoon they look like puppets rather  than living things compared Simba's  facial expressions when scar tells him  to leave and you'll notice the  difference it's also obvious during the  Stampede scene Hakuna Matata  it's not only the facial expressions  that have changed its the way the  animals move - this is most notable in  the Hakuna Matata montage where Simba  Timon and Pumbaa walk across a waterfall  while on the log  zoom in on Timon and you'll notice a  major difference in the remake he's  walking on four legs in the original  animation he walks on two legs teenage  Simba also looks much less stylized in  the remake he doesn't have those Tufts  of hair on his head and chest camera  angles do you want to see even more  side-by-sides that show the differences  between the two films when you study the  trailers for the remake you only realize  the subtle differences in camera angles  when you look real close  zoom in on Simba placing his tiny paw  and his father's big paw prints at first  glance it seems like the remake is  totally copying the original but it's  actually a different angle which is  again all about the photo realism of how  a lion would actually move new music  fans the original movie will be relieved  that their favorite tunes are back the  audience can still sing along - I just  can't wait to be king and can you feel  the love tonight of course they'll be  performed by different voice actors but  there's even more of a difference than  that there's also a brand new song by  Sir Elton John called never too late the  movie makers have kept the continuity by  using the same music legend to create  the new tune they also worked with Hans  Zimmer again who has rejiggered his  original score' humour we can expect  some new jokes too excuse me let me see  what we're dealing with that vowel  revealed that although audiences will  still  feel a connection to the animated film  they've actually taken a lot of  liberties with it and that includes the  humor he worked with his team to  understand the way memory works and what  expectations fans of the original will  have and then they were able to analyze  the animated feature and notice where  they can increase the humor they also  looked at which jokes didn't hold up as  well in this current era length Favreau  has been keen to point out that his  movie is not a shot-for-shot remake he  said that it diverges quite a bit and is  much longer than the original film why  did he need the extra time the director  explained that they've given it more  dimension not just visually but both  story wise and emotionally it's 30  minutes longer in fact the new movie has  a running time of just shy of two hours  new character our final difference  between the two movies is perhaps the  most exciting we get a brand new  character it's an elephant true voiced  by comedian Amy Sedaris these fast tiny  creatures earn their name thanks to  their long noses resembling elephant  trunks Sedaris has worked with Favreau  before on the 2014 food truck drama chef  do you think the remake will live up to  the original or not let us know in the  comments make sure to subscribe to the  things for more videos like this one and  be sure to give this video a big thumbs  up thanks for watching  [Music]

